elf by talking about her feelings  for michael or interacting with his  corpse when she thinks he's dead late in  the second movie  underworld rise of the likens is a  prequel that explains the centuries old  origins of the war between lycans and  vampires focusing on lycan lucien and  his forbidden relationship with vampire  sonia which tragically results in the  latter being killed by her father and  vampire leader victor when he discovers  that she's pregnant with a lycan child  yet even if you haven't seen the third  film in the series this story may sound  familiar that's because lucian relates  it in the first film and if that weren't  enough michael experiences it in a  series of brief flashbacks so while rise  of the likens expands on the story it's  still a rehash of a storyline that was  summed up in just a few minutes in the  first underworld  alexander corvinus passed the trait of  eternal life on to his twin sons marcus  and william who became the first vampire  and werewolf respectively while william  was unable to assume his human form ever  again the movie indicates marcus became  a vampire just like the others presented  in the franchise that is until we meet  him in underworld evolution and learn  that he has giant bat wings the apparent  result of being awoken from a lengthy  sleep by the blood of a slain likened  scientist marcus spends a majority of  the film flying around and using his  wings as weapons but it's unclear why he  has them early in the story celine tells  michael he's the first vampire-like and  hybrid and therefore there are no limits  to what he might be capable of the  question is why lichen blood would cause  marcus to sprout wings instead of  adopting more lichen-like traits in the  end it seems marcus was given his winged  advantage because he's the first vampire  but the movie never offers any real  explanation  in most vampire properties the species  is particularly vulnerable to wooden  stakes through the heart and sunlight  the underworld franchise adheres to only  half of that lore as the only thing that  will definitely take out this  franchise's vampires is sunlight in fact  vampire's vulnerability is so extreme  that their likened enemies have learned  to make uv bullets so they can destroy  vampires even when the sun is down yet  while many vampires die from exposure to  uv light throughout the series there are  also many more deaths that aren't  dependent on the sun for example victor  dies when celine lobs off half of his  head with a sword another elder amelia  dies when she is drained of blood  although there's a certain logic to  these deaths they're still somewhat  surprising more surprising still are the  deaths of two vampires in the series  second entry who selene takes out by  simply punching them and throwing them  against the wall given the strength and  durability the species is supposed to  possess it's hard to believe such a  thing would end the life of even the  weakest vampire  with the exception of underworld  awakening the series takes place in an  unnamed city in eastern europe from the  looks of the language seen on some of  the signs that city is likely in hungary  yet almost every character speaks with  an english or american accent  [Music]  how you doing most puzzling of all  however is the fact that when we first  meet michael in underworld he's clearly  american yet he's an intern at a local  hospital and the only co-worker he talks  to is a fellow american but if michael  and his co-worker want to be licensed  doctors in the united states they  wouldn't do their internships in eastern  europe perhaps the films were supposed  to feel like they could take place  anywhere but there's just enough  evidence for them being set in a  specific real world location to make the  character's accents extremely confusing  from the opening scene of the first  underworld one thing is abundantly clear  when vampire death dealers find their  likened targets their fir